<!--
    Themed-icon layer (Android 13+ Material You): the same mark as
    ic_blogmark_mark, flattened to one opaque colour for the system to tint
    from the wallpaper palette.

    Scaled to match ic_launcher_foreground's inset rather than the mark's own
    size, so the themed icon sits in the same air as the colour one — a
    monochrome layer that ran wider would jump when the user turns themed
    icons on.

    Unlike the colour mark, this can't paint the bite in a background hex —
    the OS reads only this layer's alpha to build the tint mask, so there is
    no "background colour" to erase to. The bite has to be a true alpha hole
    (fillType="evenOdd"), and — the failure mode discovered building the
    colour mark — evenOdd only produces a clean hole where the cut shape is
    *fully contained* inside the base shape; any part that falls outside
    gets filled back in as a second blob instead of vanishing. The colour
    mark's own bite (r=16, centred outside the bar on two sides) would do
    exactly that here, so this one is sized down to sit entirely inside the
    bar instead — a smaller notch than the colour version, not a mistake.
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="108dp"
    android:height="108dp"
    android:viewportWidth="108"
    android:viewportHeight="108">

    <group
        android:pivotX="54"
        android:pivotY="54"
        android:scaleX="0.74"
        android:scaleY="0.74">
        <path
            android:fillColor="#FFFFFFFF"
            android:fillType="evenOdd"
            android:pathData="M40,38 a14,14 0 0 1 28,0 v36 a14,14 0 0 1 -28,0 z M58,36 a9,9 0 0 1 18,0 a9,9 0 0 1 -18,0 z" />
    </group>
</vector>
